Two-time Oscar-winning screenwriter and director Christopher Hampton shared his insights on the film industry, AI and various latest developments at the 30th edition of the Tallinn Black Nights Film Festival (P&#xD6;FF) on Monday. He discussed his career and process during a masterclass entitled "From Stage to Screen: The Art of Adaptation" at the 25th edition of the Industry@Tallinn & Baltic Event (ITBE) market, which kicked off its Discovery Campus 2026 with the event.

Early on in his appearance, Hampton was asked about the honorary Oscar that his Dangerous Liaisons star Glenn Close just received . &#8220;About time, I think,&#8221; the British creative said in his session hosted by Estonian journalist Neeme Raud. &#8220;I was very happy to hear that. I think she &#8230; is still hoping to get one for acting, [for] a specific role, but that hasn't happened yet. So the least they could do was give her an honorary Oscar.&#8221; Related Stories Movies Kino Lorber Takes U.S. Rights to 'Edward Said: Between Worlds' (Exclusive) TV 'Slow Horses' Lands Early Renewal for Seasons 9 and 10 at Apple TV

Hampton was also asked about AI and its potential impact on creatives, especially writers. &#8220;It's not a subject, artificial intelligence, that has troubled me very much,&#8221; he shared during his masterclass. &#8220;I'm more interested in real intelligence. I think the flaw in artificial intelligence is in the name because it's artificial, and I don't think there's any replacement for original creativity. &#8230; I mean, they should put in place all these safeguards. I'm sure a lot of the work is very interesting, but for me, it's nothing other than a sort of super Google.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

What if AI replaces scribes? &#8220;I know everyone's anxious about all this, but I don't think it's going to happen,&#8221; Hampton offered. &#8220;That's all. I just don't feel it is.&#8221; And he added: &#8220;I think our work is more interesting than its work.&#8221;

Asked what he would think about AI being asked to create a script based on his past works, Hampton replied: &#8220;I really don't know. I guess it would be interesting to see what kind of a Hampton script AI would produce. But I have to leave this to other people to figure out because I can't make myself be interested in it.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

And he emphasized that he understands that AI is a hot topic. &#8220;Yeah, it's a big topic,&#8221; Hampton concluded. &#8220;It's just not with me.&#8221;

He later also suggested that young writers &#8220;may as well choose a different profession&#8221; if they want to rely on AI. &#8220;If you write a script with the help of AI, you're choosing something easy. You're being lazy. You're allowing some other external form to take over.&#8221; The film and theater veteran heads up the five-member jury for the official competition of the anniversary edition of the festival in Estonia. He is joined on the jury by Lebanese producer Myriam Sassine, U.S. cinematographer and visual effects specialist David Stump, French producer Julie Billy and Estonian actress Tiina Tauraite.

Hampton won Academy Awards for best adapted screenplay for Dangerous Liaisons and The Father . His other film credits include Atonement , The Quiet American and A Dangerous Method , while his work for the stage includes Les Liaisons Dangereuses and Sunset Boulevard . He has also written and directed three feature films: Carrington , The Secret Agent and Imagining Argentina .

In his Tallinn masterclass, Hampton emphasized that making a film based on a play is &#8220;more than simply repeating dialogue&#8221; in front of a camera, and it took him about 10 years to fully get into the right adaptation groove to feel that he had properly learned &#8220;how not to make a film from a play that smelled like the theater.&#8221; Dangerous Liaisons was the first script where he &#8220;felt like I kind of knew what I was doing,&#8221; he emphasized.

And he shared: &#8220;The mistake that I made was to think that it was easier to write films.&#8221; After all, films come with a certain amount of liberty, but this liberty &#8220;imposes on you a number of constraints,&#8221; Hampton explained. &#8220;It has its own linguistic and imagistic rules.&#8221;

The writer also shared this big-picture take on his work: &#8220;The thing about writing for film is it really is like going to the casino. You sit at the table, and you put your money on number 20, and the chances of the film being made the way you imagined it are so tiny that when it does happen, it's incredibly, incredibly fulfilling and gives you an enormous amount of pleasure. But most of the time, the croupier just takes the chips away, and that's it.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Hampton even shared that he has written scripts for films that he just can't get made. &#8220;I wrote a film quite recently, three or four years ago, about [Dmitri] Shostakovich and about his struggles as a composer in the Soviet Union,&#8221; he shared. &#8220;And I haven't been able to get it made because people don't want to engage with or want to make a film about Russia, set in Russia, dealing with Russians.&#8221; Concluded Hampton: &#8220;I find that paradoxical because, of course, it's just the time when it would be interesting to see a film about a man who struggled his whole life with the political system and somehow found ways to circumvent all the roadblocks that were put in his way.&#8221;

The writer returned to the topic of AI during the Q&A portion of his masterclass. &#8220;To go back to the beginning of the discussion, AI, I suppose everything is nudging you to always be formulaic, and of course that's what makes the studios in Hollywood happy,&#8221; Hampton argued. &#8220;They just want to tell the same stories they told last year, and I can tell you a small story about this.&#8221;

He mentioned Imagining Argentina , his 2003 historical drama starring Antonio Banderas and Emma Thompson, about political disappearances during the 1976-1983 military dictatorship in Argentina. For a preview screening, &#8220;a young audience came to see the film, and it actually went pretty well, but I'd been having trouble with the studio,&#8221; he recalled. &#8220;I'd been fighting with the producers for months, so there was a hostile atmosphere between them and me. And there was one producer standing next to me at the back, listening to the audience discussion. When the moderator asked for one word to describe the film, one audience member said &#8220;original,&#8221; Hampton recalled. &#8220;And the executive standing next to me put his face in his hands and groaned, because there's nothing more difficult for them than something that's original.&#8221;

But the veteran writer had even harsher things to say about the state of television. &#8220;I wrote a lot of television for the BBC in the '70s, and it was a very good start,&#8221; Hampton said. &#8220;And they did a lot of television plays and films in those days &#x2013; two or three a week. The paradox is that in those days there were three or four television channels in England, and you could watch twice as many television plays in a month in England as you can in a year now with 500 channels, because people don't tend to make those feature-length television films. The last one I did was in 2013 with Vanessa Redgrave and Olivia Colman.&#8221; He added: &#8220;Budgets for television are so constrained, and the rules are much more prescriptive. The last thing I did [for TV] was a series called The Singapore Grip [for ITV] about the fall of Singapore during the Second World War. It was a six-part series, it took me such a long time, and there were so many notes to negotiate from the people making [it].&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Concluded Hampton: &#8220;I just thought television really wasn't worth it anymore. Whereas it used to be, when I was young, the freest possible environment to work in, now it is the most constricted and prescriptive and unimaginative. It seems to me. And also, it's a brutal, brutal medium. It just eats material and swallows it. Obviously, it provides an enormous amount of work for writers, and to that extent, it's a good thing. But I don't really understand why the freedoms that we used to have when I was young and was very lucky to have no longer exist.&#8221;

Marrakech's Atlas Workshops heads into its ninth edition this year with upcoming films by Palestine's Leila Abbas, Morocco 's Meryem Benm' Barek and Sudan's Amjad Abu Alala in the selection and Brazilian Oscar nominee Kleber Mendon&#xE7;a Filho as its official patron.

The legacy of the project incubator, focused on films by Arab and African directors, is impressive as it approaches its 10th anniversary. To date, it has supported 242 projects, with completed films making their mark across the international festival circuit. "We calculated that since the creation of the workshops in 2018, 42 films have gone to Cannes, Venice or Berlin," says program director H&#xE9;di Zardi, who took up the role in 2023. Recent participants have included La&#xEF;la Marrakchi's Strawberries and Marie-Cl&#xE9;mentine Dusabejambo's Beni'mana , which both played in Cannes, with the latter title winning the Cam&#xE9;ra d'Or, as well as Tala Hadid's Bardi , which was selected for New York; and Rami Kodeih's Furies , which debuted in Venice and Toronto. They are among 12 films playing in Marrakech's main festival program this year that have gone through the Atlas program alongside Yassine Marco Marroccu's Alkebulan , Rani Massalha and Salem Massalha's Cairo Shadows , Abdallah Al-Khatib's Chronicles from the Siege , Rafiki Fariala's Congo Boy , Elis&#xE9; Sawasawa's Enough Is Enough , Mohammed Hammad's Safe Exit , Sara Ishaq's The Station and Mohamed Zineddaine's Until Dawn . This year's selection spans 30 feature films, selected out of 410 submissions, from 14 territories spanning Algeria, Cabo Verde, Egypt, Ethiopia, Jordan, Lebanon, Libya, Morocco, Palestine, Rwanda, Senegal, Sudan, Syria, and Tunisia. Projects in development include upcoming films from a host of directors who have already made their mark, including Abu Alala ( You Will Die at Twenty ), Abbas ( Thank You for Banking with Us ), Benm'Barek ( Behind the Palm Trees ), Sofia Alaoui ( Animalia ), and Yasmine Benkiran ( Queens ). Abu Alala will present A Beautiful Land To Die, about a man on death row who suddenly finds himself free when Kober Central Prison is broken open in the early days of Sudan's ongoing war, and Abbas attends with New York-set A merica Is Behind The Door about a Palestinian woman trying to carve out a new life in the U.S. Alaoui is in the selection with The Sleepers about a doctor on a mission to get to the bottom of a mysterious sleeping sickness afflicting her town, while Benm' Barek brings Ashes, an allegorical drama exploring the legacy of human rights abuses during Morocco's Years Of Lead. Benkiran will present Waltz of the Savages about a July 14 Bastille Day imposed upon the Pasha of an Arab country under French colonial rule in 1955, as its citizens clamor for independence outside the palace walls. Newcomers to the lab include Syrian-Germany director Dyaa Naim's Chronicles of an Announced Death , about an undertaker living as a refugee in Germany on a mission to return his father's body to Syria following the fall of the Bashir Al-Assad regime, while Zardi also highlights Studio K&#xE9;b&#xE9; , the first film from Senegalese director Dyana Gaye in more than a decade. Four of the 11 projects being presented at the production or post-production stage attended while in development, spanning Rwandan director Samuel Ishimwe's Seasons of the Weary Kind , Senagalese Yoro Mbaye's Fagadaga and Alaa Eddine Aljem's Eldorado Isma&#xEB;l El Iraki's Wolfmother , which both hail from Morocco. Related Stories News Festival In Focus: Marrakech Navigates Turbulent World To Celebrate Established Cinema Artists & Newcomers News Jeff Goldblum, John Turturro & Younès Mégri To Be Honored At Marrakech; Rian Johnson & James Gray Set For Conversations

Discoveries Discoveries include the workshop's first Libyan production, 1986 by Mouayed Zabtia. The coming-of-age tale follows a young man who has to hide his love of Western music when hostilities breakout between Libya and the U.S. "We like to nurture strong links with directors but we also like discoveries," says Zardi, explaining that Zabtia is a self-taught director who learned about the workshops on the social networks and sent in his project on spec. "He identified two labs which included us. I couldn't believe what I was seeing. I asked myself, &#8220;Does he actually exist?&#8221; I felt the need to call him, just to ask, &#8220;Is it a real person?,&#8221; recounts Zardi blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

&#8220;I learned that he had worked in television doing a lot of series following the fall of the Gaddafi regime and that he had set aside some money and bought equipment to make his film.&#8221;

Another five projects will be presented on in the Atlas Close-Ups section dedicated to Moroccan cinema, including B514, The Man Who Forgot To Die by Jihane el Bahhar, who achieved box office success at home with last film Triple A. Eight prizes will be awarded across the three sections, with the development jury comprising producers Nadim Cheikrouha ( The Voice of Hind Rajab ), Marie-Ange Luciani ( Anatomy of a Fall ) and Linda Mutawi ( Eagles of the Republic ).

Distributor Eddie Bertozzi at Be Water Film, director Mohamed Kordofani and BFI London Film Festival Artistic Director Kristy Matheson will decide the post-production awards. With the Atlas Workshops at its heart, Marrakech's industry offering restructured last year under the Atlas Programs banner to now encompass Atlas Station, focused on up-skilling 10 Moroccan professionals, and the Atlas Distribution strand. The latter, aimed at encouraging the circulation of films in the Middle East and Africa, comprises the Atlas Distribution Awards feting proactive distributors in the region, and the Atlas Distribution Meetings, bringing some 60 distribution professionals from Africa, the Arab world, and Europe to Marrakech to screen films in the festival program and the Atlas Workshops post-production showcase. Zardi is also quietly looking to expand the attendance of private investors, with a handful checking out th event as observers this year. Its a move that makes sense, he says, with funding out of Europe under pressure, while opportunities are growing in MENA and Africa.

Bespoke Approach blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Amid the growth, Zardi is determined to keep the workshop activities boutique and bespoke, with much of the activity increasingly revolving around one-on-ones and small group meetings, while all of the attendees will participate in sessions with The Secret Agent director Mendon&#xE7;a Filho. He follows in the footsteps of Cristian Mungiu and Jeff Nichols, who previously took on the recently created role of patron under which he will mentor the attendees, who will then take on the mantle of being of &#8220;The Year of Kleber Mendon&#xE7;a Filho&#8221;.

"I think of the reasons the workshops work is because we try to avoid too many generalized, theoretical panels to focus more on one-on-ones or small group meetings,&#8221; says Zardi. The 11 mentors this year will include director Cl&#xE9;mentine Dusajembo, John Trengove and actors Nisrin Erradi, Majd Mastoura and Lyna Khoudri, while experts and consultants include editor Nadia Ben Rachid, colorist Micheal Derrosett and sound editor Rana Eid. "We are pushing more and more towards this way of working. A number of directors with films in the festival program will be coming to meet with the projects," explains Zardi. "So, we'll have a director and an actor, a director and a cinematographer, or two directors in conversation with one another. And a new step we've taken this year is organizing preparatory sessions with all the consultants, briefing them on the material that has been sent in." The actors, for example, will be working with the directors on questions around the dynamics of a character's personality. "I feel it's more valuable, within the broader industry dynamic, where one-on-ones are often geared toward future project financing to place a strong emphasis on making these interactions concrete, hands-on working sessions."

Sundance prize winners &#8220;Josephine&#8221; and &#8220;The Incomer,&#8221; along with Berlin Silver Bear winner &#8220;Queen at Sea,&#8221; are among the films set to screen at the third edition of Tribeca Festival Lisboa, which will run Dec. 9-13 in the Portuguese capital.

The five-day festival will feature a slate of international and Portuguese films, alongside conversations and special events with Bob Odenkirk, Alec Baldwin, Isla Fisher, Zosia Mamet, Edward Burns, Zach Woods and W. Kamau Bell. Screenings and events will take place across several venues along Lisbon's Avenida da Liberdade. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Taika Waititi Slams ‘Klara and the Sun’ Critics as ‘Irrelevant,’ ‘Sad Lost Souls’ and Says He Hasn’t ‘Read a Review’ in 15 Years: 'But I Do Listen to the People’

Curated by Tribeca Festival director and SVP of programming Cara Cusumano, the international lineup includes Beth de Ara&#xFA;jo's &#8220;Josephine,&#8221; which won both the U.S. dramatic grand jury prize and audience award at the 2026 Sundance Film Festival; Lance Hammer's &#8220;Queen at Sea,&#8221; which received the Silver Bear Jury Prize at the Berlin International Film Festival; and Louis Paxton's &#8220;The Incomer,&#8221; winner of Sundance's NEXT Innovator Award.

The program also features several selections from this year's Tribeca Festival in New York, including Burns' &#8220;Finnegan's Foursome,&#8221; which he directed and stars in; &#8220;The Accompanist,&#8221; the feature directorial debut of Woods; and Joshua Z. Weinstein's &#8220;Here I'm Alive,&#8221; which received a special jury mention for best U.S. narrative feature.

Rounding out the international slate are Rob Burnett's &#8220;In Memoriam,&#8221; Rachel Rose's &#8220;The Last Day&#8221; and Michael LaHaie's &#8220;Bob and David Climb Machu Picchu,&#8221; featuring Odenkirk. Odenkirk, Burns, Woods, Weinstein and Burnett are expected to attend screenings and participate in conversations about their films. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;Following a banner year in New York, it's especially meaningful to see the festival's spirit taking root in Lisbon, with audiences who are as curious and passionate about storytelling as we are,&#8221; Cusumano said in a statement. &#8220;Our program brings together established voices and exciting new filmmakers, with films that are funny, moving, and deeply personal.&#8221;

Portuguese cinema will also be represented through a dedicated program curated by Rui Tendinha. Selections include Vicente Alves do &#xD3;'s &#8220;O Dia Mais Feliz da Minha Vida,&#8221; Ant&#xF3;nio Borges Correia's &#8220;Tamb&#xE9;m Tu Clara,&#8221; Lu&#xED;s Galv&#xE3;o Teles' &#8220;Vindima,&#8221; Cl&#xE1;udio Jord&#xE3;o's &#8220;Viagens entre Estrelas e Ondas,&#8221; Tekla Taidell's &#8220;6:06&#8221; and B&#xE1;rbara Tinoco's documentary &#8220;Mulherzinha.&#8221; Portuguese shorts include &#8220;Bom Dia (Uma Esp&#xE9;cie de Ora&#xE7;&#xE3;o)&#8221; from Diogo Brito, &#8220;My Type&#8221; from returning festival ambassador Daniela Ruah and &#8220;Prova de Cor&#8221; from Joana Botelho.

The festival is also expanding its live programming with the introduction of Tribeca Night Live, a series of events combining comedy, conversation and music. Bell, Ruah and Portuguese comedian Lu&#xED;s Franco-Bastos will host the evening programming, while musical offerings include &#8220;Xmas with Mozart,&#8221; marking the 270th anniversary of the composer's birth, and an audiovisual performance by Portuguese electronic musician Moullinex and his MXGPU project.

Now in its third year, Tribeca Festival Lisboa serves as an international extension of the New York-based festival, with an emphasis on independent and awards-season cinema. Its inaugural edition in 2024 hosted the Portuguese premiere of Sean Baker's eventual best picture Oscar winner &#8220;Anora,&#8221; while the 2025 edition featured Portuguese premieres of Yorgos Lanthimos' &#8220;Bugonia&#8221; and Mary Bronstein's &#8220;If I Had Legs I'd Kick You.&#8221;

&#8220;Tribeca Festival Lisboa is an important part of how we're extending Tribeca's reach and connecting with audiences around the world,&#8221; said Rebecca Glashow, CEO of Tribeca Enterprises. &#8220;This partnership reflects our broader vision of bringing artists, audiences, and creative communities together in new ways, while remaining rooted in Lisbon's own creative community.&#8221;

The festival will take place at Cinema S&#xE3;o Jorge, Teatro Tivoli BBVA and Capit&#xF3;lio. It is co-organized by Tribeca Enterprises, Impresa and Lisboa Cultura, with support from the Lisbon City Council and several corporate and tourism partners.

Goldfinger got sucked out of an airplane window. Dr. No was boiled to death in a radioactive reactor chamber. And Kananga? He got force-fed a compressed-gas bullet and blew up like a human Hindenburg.

While the whole world has been obsessing over who'll get picked as the next James Bond , there hasn't been nearly enough attention paid to the truly critical role &#x2014; who'll get to die in a spectacularly outlandish way as the next Bond villain.

They seldom get the girl &#x2014; and even when they do, they usually push her into a pool full of piranhas &#x2014; but the bad guys in Bond movies are every bit as vital to the franchise as 007 himself. Maybe even more so. The fact is, James Bond will be James Bond no matter who plays him. Whether it's Jack Lowden, Callum Turner or Josh O'Connor &#x2014; or anybody else who might be screen-testing right now in London &#x2014; you can bet the bank in Monte Carlo that they'll all portray the character roughly the same way: coolly bemused, supremely confident and elegantly ruthless. As different as George Lazenby was from Sean Connery, and Timothy Dalton was from Roger Moore, and Daniel Craig was from Pierce Brosnan, they all inhabited the same canonically tailored tuxedo. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Movies James Bond Found: Robert Pattinson to Play 007 Movies From Sandworms to 007: Joe Walker on 'Dune: Part Three,' Villeneuve, McQueen and Bond

The Bond villains, on the other hand, are unique to every film &#x2014; they give each movie its distinctive personality. While 007 remains fixed, there's no limit to how flamboyant or grotesque or ridiculous his nemeses can be. They can have metal hands, golden guns, bleeding eyes or even, for no particular reason, diamonds stuck into their faces. They live in private volcanoes, wear poison-tipped shoes and keep pet sharks in their swimming pools. And they get to deliver the best dialogue &#x2014; &#8220;See that some harm comes to him&#8221; &#x2014; usually while feeding people to crocodiles or performing a circumcision with a laser beam. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;A Bond movie is only as good as its villain,&#8221; notes veteran Bond writer Bruce Feirstein, who helped pen GoldenEye and other Brosnan-era films. &#8220;Think back to what you remember about your favorite Bond movies &#x2014; it's the villain. They're the most operatic characters. They're the most fun.&#8221;

They also tend to be the trickiest to cast.

So far, there hasn't been much chatter about who director Denis Villeneuve might have in mind for Bond 26. One British tabloid reported a rumor that Ewan Mitchell, the British actor who plays Aemond Targaryen on House of the Dragon , was under consideration. But at 29, Mitchell seems awfully young to pull off Ernst Stavro Blofeld or any of the other megalomaniacal masterminds 007 typically faces off against. There's certainly no shortage of other actors offering their services, including 51-year-old David Dastmalchian (whom Villeneuve has cast before, in Prisoners , Blade Runner 2049 and Dune ) and 59-year-old Paul Giamatti (&#8220;I'd definitely want an accent,&#8221; he told GQ in 2023, adding that &#8220;it'd be nice to have an animal with me of some kind&#8221;). Kalshi, meanwhile, is offering its own odds for Bond baddies, putting Cillian Murphy at 28 percent, Idris Elba at 24, Barry Keoghan at 18 and &#x2014; here's one with potential! &#x2014; Peter Dinklage at 15.

Since it's unclear whether Peaky Blinders scribe Steven Knight has finished the Bond script he's been writing for Villeneuve, it might still be a while before a genuine search begins, even if recent reports that a Bond decision is imminent turn out to be true. Casting Bond without a storyline, after all, is easier than casting the character who'll give him one. But when the hunt does start, there are a few things Villeneuve might want to keep in mind. Because the best Bond villains, the ones everyone tends to remember, have some key qualities in common. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

For starters, look for an actor who can pull off an eye patch, facial scars or an immaculately shaved head. Bond, whoever plays him, is always classically handsome and impeccably wardrobed. But the actors portraying his adversary do best when they physically project villainy in odd, indelible ways, whether it's Joseph Wiseman's prosthetic claws or Javier Bardem's shockingly atrocious dental work. Impeccable manners are also important. The best Bond villains don't snarl or rant or tear open their Nehru jackets in melodramatic meltdowns. They invite 007 to dinner, pour the wine, show him around the underwater lair while politely explaining their plans for destroying the world. For that, you don't need a Brando-like method actor; you need someone who can make courteous chitchat feel homicidal.

